12111	{"artifactId":"HLD-315d510cb28100e2","entry":{"id":"migrated-HLD-315d510cb28100e2-review","actor":{"id":"insrc:daemon","kind":"agent","properties":{}},"timestamp":"2026-10-16T19:04:24.273Z","operation":"review","outcome":{"result":"block","summary":"review: block","data":{"kind":"review","report":{"artifact":"design.epic","stage":"design.epic","verdict":"block","findings":[{"claimId":"coverage-of-intent.1","ref":"coverage-of-intent","item":"coverage-of-intent","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"Every DEF acceptance criterion ac1–ac38 is assigned to a Story, and the HLD builds none of the DEF non-goals (no provisional mode, no automatic takeover, no shared daemon, no polling or webhooks, no working-tree policy, no GitLab or Jira adapters).","evidence":"DEF.md lists ac1–ac38 (lines 130–244). The HLD test map assigns ac1–4 to s1, ac5–9 to s2, ac10–14 to s3, ac15–18 to s4, ac19–23 to s5, ac25–28 to s6, ac24 and ac29–35 to s7, and ac36–38 to s8. The non-goals (DEF §3, lines 84–93) are respected: ReleaseRule refuses others (no takeover), completion is read on demand through readSignoffState (no polling), the policy is read only from the remote default branch, a3 (shared daemon) is rejected, and only the port is built while GitLab and Jira are deferred. Git-pull sync is explicitly deferred to a define extend, which matches the recorded resolution of q8baf0b8f. [files: docs/epics/team-signoff-claim-based-workflow-E20261016315d510c/DEF.md]","action":"none","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"current-behaviour.1","ref":"current-behaviour","item":"current-behaviour","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"approveArtifactByJsonPath is synchronous, signals refusal only by throwing (ReviewBlockedError), and approveWorkflowTarget catches only ReviewBlockedError into skipped[] and rethrows everything else.","evidence":"gates.ts:584 `export function approveArtifactByJsonPath(jsonPath, opts?): ApprovalResult` is synchronous and throws ReviewBlockedError at 610/624 before writeAtomic at 654. gates.ts:865-873: `if (err instanceof ReviewBlockedError) { skipped.push(...) } else { throw err; }`. So the new TeamGateRefusedError must be added there, as the design says. [files: src/workflow/gates.ts]","action":"none","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"current-behaviour.2","ref":"current-behaviour","item":"current-behaviour","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The terminal client's approve case reaches approveArtifactByJsonPath in-process through svc.workflow.approve and formats only an error named ReviewBlockedError.","evidence":"cli/services/workflow.ts:158-160 calls approveArtifactByJsonPath directly. cli/command.ts:229-233: `if (err instanceof Error && err.name === 'ReviewBlockedError') { return [...] } throw err;`. [files: src/cli/services/workflow.ts, src/cli/command.ts]","action":"none","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"current-behaviour.3","ref":"current-behaviour","item":"current-behaviour","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The review paths differ: findings.ts writes the body back untouched (meta-only), while verdicts.ts and run-artifact.ts apply auto-fixes and rewrite the body and the markdown.","evidence":"findings.ts:80-92 says 'the body is written back untouched' and spreads `...artifact` with only meta.review changed. verdicts.ts:93-98 calls applyAutoFixes and then writeAtomic on the json and md. run-artifact.ts:105-121 builds `body: fixed.body` and writes both files. [files: src/daemon/steps/review-step/phases/findings.ts, src/daemon/steps/review-step/phases/verdicts.ts, src/workflow/review/run-artifact.ts]","action":"none","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"new-versus-reuse.1","ref":"new-versus-reuse","item":"new-versus-reuse","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"whoAmI can reuse the existing ghCurrentLogin, and the existing GitHub calls run synchronously, so a synchronous port is feasible without changing approveArtifactByJsonPath's signature.","evidence":"tracker/github.ts:408 `export function ghCurrentLogin(): string`. All gh and git calls go through `_exec = execFileSync` (lines 17-36). There is no timeout option today; the 10-second wrapper is correctly listed as new s1 work. [files: src/workflow/tracker/github.ts]","action":"none","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"new-versus-reuse.2","ref":"new-versus-reuse","item":"new-versus-reuse","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"Tracker settings already live in a per-repo map in ~/.insrc/github.json, read by resolveGithubConfig.","evidence":"config/github.ts:93 `readonly repos?: Readonly<Record<string, GithubEntry>>`, :144 `join(PATHS.insrc, 'github.json')`, and :196-198 `resolveGithubConfig(repoPath, ...)` reads `file.repos?.[repoPath]`. [files: src/workflow/config/github.ts]","action":"none","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"change-sites.1","ref":"change-sites","item":"change-sites","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The inventory of non-test writeAtomic callers (19 files including code-review-step handler.ts and code-review/runner.ts) and the two writeFileSync artifact writers is complete, and bugfix/tracker.ts writes through patchTrackerMeta.","evidence":"A grep for `writeAtomic(` outside __tests__ found 19 files: repo-ignore-config, workflow-rpc, verdicts, findings, synthesize, link, refs, storage (the definition), questions, gates, amendments/store, resolve-comment, tracker-auto, bugfix/stamp, orchestrator, run-artifact, review/resolve, standalone-record and test-record. code-review-step/handler.ts:131 and code-review/runner.ts:150 pass writeAtomic by reference (`write: writeAtomic`), so the design's list matches. provenance/writer.ts:101 and migrate-docs-tree.ts:856 use writeFileSync. bugfix/tracker.ts:387 calls `patchTrackerMeta(json, { issueRef: ref })`. [files: src/workflow/storage.ts, src/daemon/steps/code-review-step/handler.ts, src/workflow/code-review/runner.ts, src/workflow/artifacts/provenance/writer.ts, src/workflow/migrate-docs-tree.ts, src/workflow/bugfix/tracker.ts]","action":"none","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"change-sites.2","ref":"change-sites","item":"change-sites","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"No caller signature changes, and roughly 70 test call sites of approveArtifactByJsonPath stay unaffected.","evidence":"The grep counted 81 `approveArtifactByJsonPath(` occurrences across 27 test files, which is about 70 call sites net of comments and imports. The gate is invoked inside the function and reads the user itself (sc4 TeamGate takes no `who`), so the signature at gates.ts:584 is unchanged. [files: src/workflow/gates.ts]","action":"none","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"data-compatibility.1","ref":"data-compatibility","item":"data-compatibility","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The existing TrackerMeta fields are kept unchanged, only the adapter literal 'github' is widened, and meta.revision and tracker.units are new keys that collide with nothing.","evidence":"tracker/refs.ts:24-37 TrackerMeta has exactly adapter?: 'github', epicRef, storyRef, issueRef, storyRefs, taskRefs, milestoneRef, labelsCreated, epicStatus, storyStatus, pushedAt and lastSyncedAt, which matches sc3's ExistingTrackerMeta. A grep for `meta.revision`, `revision?:` and `units?:` under src/workflow found no matches, so the new keys collide with nothing. [files: src/workflow/tracker/refs.ts]","action":"none","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"boundaries.1","ref":"boundaries","item":"boundaries","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"Each shared contract has one owner Story, consumers depend only on owners that land earlier, and the change to the terminal client's approve case (command.ts) is owned by s5 alone.","evidence":"sc1→s1, sc2→s2, sc3→s4, sc4→s5, sc5→s7, and the rollout phases A to D land each owner before its consumers. s5 avoids depending on sc5 because the completion hook ships unregistered and s7 registers it. The command.ts and approveWorkflowTarget changes appear only in s5's boundary. s3 and s1 share the github.json store, which is stated explicitly. [files: docs/epics/team-signoff-claim-based-workflow-E20261016315d510c/DEF.md]","action":"none","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"error-paths.1","ref":"error-paths","item":"error-paths","kind":"semantic","severity":"MED","premise":"The design states how a repo with no policy is told apart from a repo whose policy cannot be fetched, so that the gate placed inside approveArtifactByJsonPath stays a no-op for solo, offline, remote-less or non-git repos.","evidence":"The HLD says 'In a repo without a policy the gate is skipped and behaviour is as today' and 'Without a policy file no team check is made (ac7)'. It also adopts ac9's offline rule: when the remote default branch cannot be reached, use a cached policy if one exists, otherwise refuse the action. PolicyReader returns `{ ok:false; reason }` on failure, separate from the 'none' value. Nowhere does the design say what the gate does on `ok:false` when no cached copy exists and the repo has never had a policy (offline, no remote, or not a git repo). The approval path runs in non-git temp dirs: gates.ts:575-578 already tolerates 'not a git repo, no commits yet, or git unavailable' for headFullSha, and the ~70 test callers use temp repos. If the reader's ok:false is treated as a refusal, as ac9 reads, every approval in a remote-less or offline solo repo is refused, which breaks k4 and ac7. The design also does not say whether reading the remote default branch requires a fetch (network) on every guarded action; the NFT budgets only the tracker reads. [files: src/workflow/gates.ts, docs/epics/team-signoff-claim-based-workflow-E20261016315d510c/DEF.md]","action":"In sc2 and s2, define the outcomes explicitly: no remote, not a git repo, or no policy file on a cached or fetched ref → 'none' (no team checks); a known team repo whose policy cannot be refreshed → cached copy, otherwise refuse. Record in the cache that a repo is a team repo, so a repo is treated as 'unknown' only when it has never been seen to carry a policy. State whether the default-branch read fetches, and bound its timeout in the non-functional targets.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"does-not-hold"},{"claimId":"error-paths.2","ref":"error-paths","item":"error-paths","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"A refusal at local approval is detectable and handled without partial stamping or aborting a batch.","evidence":"The design throws TeamGateRefusedError before any stamp. In today's code all throws in approveArtifactByJsonPath happen before writeAtomic at gates.ts:654, so a gate placed in that region preserves the no-partial-stamp property. Routing the error to skipped[] beside ReviewBlockedError (gates.ts:868) keeps a batch approve going. [files: src/workflow/gates.ts]","action":"none","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"tests.1","ref":"tests","item":"tests","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"Each acceptance criterion maps to a test in a named Story, and the design-specific risks (gate sites, batch refusal, terminal formatting, write-helper bypass, report-only review on both paths, unreadable identity) each have a test.","evidence":"The HLD test map covers ac1–ac38 across s1–s8 with no gaps (checked against DEF.md lines 130–244). It adds a refusal test per gate site, a batch-approve skipped[] test, a test of the terminal client's approve case, a bypass test that covers writeFileSync, a report-only review test on both review paths, an unreadable-identity test, and pinned-results tests for ac3. [files: docs/epics/team-signoff-claim-based-workflow-E20261016315d510c/DEF.md]","action":"none","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"}],"counts":{"high":0,"med":1,"low":12,"unverified":0},"template":"design-spec","reviewedAt":"2026-10-16T19:04:24.273Z","model":"cli-claude:opus","reviewedBy":"daemon"},"resolutions":{}}}}}
